WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.587
NULLIF is a small function with many practical uses.

00:00:03.587 --> 00:00:08.642
It compares two expressions. If they are equal, it returns NULL.

00:00:08.642 --> 00:00:11.742
Otherwise, it returns the first expression.

00:00:11.742 --> 00:00:18.197
For text cleaning, a useful pattern is to trim a field and then compare it with an empty

00:00:18.197 --> 00:00:22.737
string. If the cleaned text is empty, NULLIF converts it to NULL.

00:00:22.737 --> 00:00:27.462
This allows us to treat empty and whitespace-only text as missing.

00:00:27.462 --> 00:00:34.602
For example, a preferred-language field might contain three spaces because a user clicked into the field and

00:00:34.602 --> 00:00:38.597
saved without typing anything. After trimming, that becomes an empty string.

00:00:38.597 --> 00:00:42.937
NULLIF can turn it into NULL so later logic becomes simpler.

00:00:42.937 --> 00:00:50.677
NULLIF is also useful when a particular numeric value should be treated specially.

00:00:50.677 --> 00:00:54.237
The classic example is a denominator of zero.

00:00:54.237 --> 00:00:58.647
NULLIF can convert exactly zero to NULL before division, preventing a division-by-zero error.

00:00:58.647 --> 00:01:05.072
What I like about NULLIF is that it makes the rule visible.

00:01:05.072 --> 00:01:10.292
We are saying: if this value equals a particular sentinel or invalid value,

00:01:10.292 --> 00:01:13.042
treat it as missing for this calculation.

00:01:13.042 --> 00:01:15.887
It is not a universal cleaning function.

00:01:15.887 --> 00:01:20.158
Use it when two values being equal should intentionally produce NULL.
